---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Island — Phase 0">
  <div data-scroll-root>
    <!-- The hero IS the drag surface, matching the reference site's pattern:
         one element carries data-cursor and owns --slide-progress. -->
    <section class="hero" data-cursor="draggable" data-chapter>
      <div>
        <h1 data-reveal data-parallax="1.6">
          An island<br /><em>turning</em> in the light
        </h1>
        <p data-reveal data-parallax="1.2">Phase 0 · pipeline proof</p>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-hint">
        <span>Drag horizontally</span>
        <span>slide <b data-slide-readout>0.00</b></span>
        <span>Scroll to approach</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" data-chapter>
      <div class="panel" data-parallax="0.5">
        <div class="eyebrow" data-reveal>01 — Light</div>
        <h2 data-reveal>The sun is a single source of truth</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          Dragging sweeps one scalar. That scalar sets the sun's direction and
          the whole palette — sky, terrain, water and fog all read the same
          uniforms, so the scene re-lights as one coherent world instead of
          several effects animating side by side.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          The camera orbits at a different rate than the sun sweeps. If they
          matched, the light would be pinned to the viewer and never appear to
          move across the island.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" data-chapter>
      <div class="panel" data-parallax="0.5">
        <div class="eyebrow" data-reveal>02 — Water</div>
        <h2 data-reveal>The piece a mountain scene never needs</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          The surface is displaced by layered swells, and its normals come from
          the same height function via finite differences — so shading can
          never disagree with the geometry.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          Fresnel weights an analytic sky reflection, a sharp specular term
          gives sun glitter, and foam builds on wave crests and in a shoreline
          band that breaks against the coast.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" data-chapter>
      <div class="panel" data-parallax="0.5">
        <div class="eyebrow" data-reveal>03 — Next</div>
        <h2 data-reveal>Swap the placeholder, keep the pipeline</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          The terrain here is procedural noise standing in for the artist's
          island model. Phase 2 replaces that one function with a GLTF load and
          a baked lightmap — every contract around it already works.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="foot">Drag · Scroll · Navigate — the canvas never reloads</footer>
  </div>
</Layout>
